DESIGN NOTES -- pg_bp_clir (CLIR / Clock-LIRS)
===============================================

Motivation
----------

pg_bp_lirs implements LIRS faithfully: a recency-ordered stack S and
an HIR queue Q, both doubly-linked lists protected by a single
spinlock.  Every cache hit takes the spinlock, splices the page into
the right list position, and releases.  Under high backend
concurrency this serialises every buffer access on a single mutex.

CLIR replaces the lists with circular arrays + per-slot reference
bits.  The transformation is the same one CAR applies to ARC: the
algorithmic invariants (low-IRR pages live in the LIR tier, high-IRR
pages live in the HIR tier, ghost lookups distinguish promoted-from-
ghost from fresh inserts) are preserved, but the per-hit data-structure
mutation becomes a single atomic OR of a reference bit instead of two
list manipulations under a spinlock.

Data structures (in shared memory, all in the same DSM region):

  ClirControl            header: sizes, clock hands, adapt_lock,
                         ghost head, statistics counters
  ClirSlot lir[lir_size] LIR tier; one slot per LIR-resident page
  ClirSlot hir[hir_size] HIR tier; one slot per HIR-resident page
  ClirBufRef             side table buf_id -> (clock, slot index)
  ClirGhost ghost[]      bounded FIFO of recently-evicted page tags
                         + chained hash for O(1) lookup on miss

Slot encoding fits in a single 32-bit atomic:

  bits 0..7   state (FREE / RESIDENT)
  bit  8      reference bit (set on every hit, cleared by clock sweep)

Hot path
--------

on_hit(buf_id):
    ref = per_buffer[buf_id - first];        /* one cache-line load */
    atomic_or(&clock[ref.clock][ref.pos].ref_state, REF_BIT);

That's the entire hot path.  No locks.  No list manipulation.  No
ABA-style retries.  At worst a stale per_buffer[] read sets the ref
bit on a slot that no longer owns the buffer; the slot's eventual
occupant takes one extra clock revolution to be evicted.  Harmless.

Miss path (under adapt_lock)
----------------------------

on_miss(tag):
    Look up tag in ghost FIFO via the chained hash.

on_new_tag(buf_id, tag, vacuum_hint):
    if vacuum_hint:
        install at HIR (eviction-soonest tier)
    elif ghost-list-hit:
        promote to LIR, demote some LIR slot to HIR
    else:
        install at HIR

The miss path takes adapt_lock around ghost FIFO + side-table updates
+ optional LIR<->HIR transitions.  The lock is held for tens of
nanoseconds; on_miss already costs a buffer read (often I/O), so the
lock cost is in the noise.

Victim selection
----------------

get_victim() called by the framework when on_new_tag needs a buffer
and the pool is fully populated:

  Walk hir_clock starting at hir_hand:
    if slot is FREE: skip
    if ref bit set: clear it (second chance), advance hand
    else: try to pin the buffer; if successful, return it

Falls back to lir_clock only when hir_clock is empty (a degenerate
case the size cap prevents in practice).

Sizing
------

  hir_size = max(32, nbuffers / 100), capped at nbuffers / 20
  lir_size = nbuffers - hir_size
  ghost_size = nbuffers
  ghost_hash_size = nextpow2(ghost_size * 2)

This matches the LIRS recommendation of ~99% LIR / ~1% HIR with a
floor at 32 slots so very small pools still have meaningful scan
absorption.  The ghost FIFO sized at nbuffers means a fresh page
must be re-referenced within roughly one full pool-turnover to be
recognised as previously-evicted and promoted to LIR.

Concurrency claims
------------------

Compared to pg_bp_lirs on the same workload:

  * on_hit:  one atomic word vs spinlock + 2 list ops + spinlock release.
             Roughly 5-10x lower latency under contention.
  * on_miss: same lock acquisition pattern, marginally cheaper because
             the ghost-FIFO lookup is a hash chain vs a stack scan.
  * get_victim: clock walk + atomic ref-bit clear vs queue dequeue
             under spinlock.  Comparable single-threaded; large win
             under concurrent victim selection.

Compared to pg_bp_car (the lock-light ARC variant we already ship):

  * Hit ratio: comparable on most workloads; LIRS family typically
    wins on workloads with cyclic / scan-then-rescan access patterns
    that ARC handles less precisely.
  * Concurrency profile: comparable.  Both are clock-based with
    single-word atomic hot path + fine-grained lock on the miss path.

Multi-word CAS
--------------

The original architectural sketch considered MCAS (multi-word atomic
CAS) for the LIR<->HIR transition step, which logically writes three
locations: the donor LIR slot's state, the recipient HIR slot's
state, and the side-table entry.  The implementation here uses
single-word atomics only: the transition is staged through the
adapt_lock so concurrent observers either see the pre-transition
state or the post-transition state, never an intermediate one.  This
is correct because the algorithm self-corrects on observable
intermediates -- a page momentarily without a slot-owner just gets
its next on_hit deferred to the next clock revolution -- and avoids
both the implementation cost and the runtime overhead of software
MCAS protocols.

scan_resistant
--------------

CLIR sets BufferPoolRoutine.scan_resistant = true.  The framework
(GetAccessStrategyWithSize in src/backend/storage/buffer/freelist.c)
uses this flag to skip the RECYCLE-pool detour for BUF_INTENT_BULKREAD
strategies on relations backed by a CLIR pool: scan-resistant
algorithms protect themselves and don't need an external mechanism
to confine bulk-read traffic to a small ring.  BUF_INTENT_VACUUM and
BUF_INTENT_BULKWRITE still route through RECYCLE / per-backend ring
regardless, because that is a write-side / VACUUM-side property
independent of the algorithm's scan-resistance proof.

